    <div class="founding-body">
      <p>We have always believed that health is only half the equation.</p>
      <p>A member who trains well and goes home to an empty calendar has solved the easier problem. The harder one, the one most adults quietly avoid asking themselves, is what to do with the energy once you have it. Where to spend a sharp mind. Who to spend an evening with. How to keep growing, intellectually and socially, long after most people have stopped trying.</p>
      <p>The Enrichment Program is the Club&rsquo;s answer. A standing calendar of dinners, gatherings, conversations, and the occasional departure from Tulsa entirely, built around the same three pillars as everything else we do: the body, the mind, and the company we keep.</p>
      <p>It is not optional, exactly. It simply tends to become essential, in the way that good things do once you&rsquo;ve made a habit of them.</p>
      <p>Some of what&rsquo;s on it: an annual tennis tournament that has run for over a century. A private chef dinner with no fixed guest list and no fixed agenda. A forum, occasionally, with someone worth listening to. A migration south each winter, for the half of the methodology most members are worst at.</p>
      <p class="coda">The full calendar is reserved for members. We find that&rsquo;s part of what makes it worth joining.</p>
